    "title": "What port spreadsheets taught me about transfers",
    "body": "Field report from the manifest-tracking experiment. For three weeks I have been mapping my port spreadsheets onto the sandbox data model, and the overlap is almost embarrassing. Lessons that transferred directly: 1) One immutable reference per shipment or transfer. At the port, the moment a container gets a second “corrected” number, you have lost it — same for money. 2) Status fields lie unless someone is accountable for their staleness; our manifests only became trustworthy when the gate clerk had to initial every status change. In API terms: every state transition needs an author and a timestamp, no silent updates. 3) Reconciliation is not a report you run at the end — it is a habit you run daily, or the errors compound past repair. I converted my three best sheet templates into test fixtures: 60 fake transfers with realistic messiness (duplicates, out-of-order webhooks, one typo’d reference). Ayaan’s API digested everything except the typo case, which now has a ticket. Spreadsheets: still undefeated as a thinking tool.",
