    "authorHandle": "abshir_maal",
    "title": "How I evaluate a restaurant P&L in 20 minutes",
    "body": "Members asked, so here is the 20-minute version I have used across three restaurants of my own and a dozen I looked at and walked away from. Minutes 0-5: food cost as a share of sales. In our kind of restaurant, if it is under a quarter I assume the numbers are wrong; if it is over forty percent I assume the kitchen is being robbed — politely ask which one it is. Minutes 5-10: rent against sales. Past fifteen percent the location is eating the business, and no amount of good cooking outruns a bad lease. Minutes 10-15: the payroll line — count the family members working who do not appear in it. Unpaid family labour is a hidden subsidy, and a P&L should show the business, not the sacrifice. Minutes 15-20: cash versus recorded sales. Sit near the till for one lunch service, count covers, multiply by the average plate, compare to the book. The gap tells you more than the statements do. None of this replaces proper diligence — it tells you whether proper diligence is worth your next twenty hours.",
