    "oneLiner": "A fake-money practice environment where Somali builders can safely design, test, and prove remittance tools before touching real rails.",
    "problem": "Remittances are the financial backbone of Somali families, yet the tools around them barely change. Anyone who wants to improve the experience faces an impossible choice: experiment on live money rails — with real families’ transfers, licensing exposure, and compliance risk — or build blind, with no realistic environment to test against. The result is a graveyard of half-finished remittance apps, agents who cannot safely evaluate new software, and an industry still running on paper workflows and trust that technology keeps failing to earn.",
    "solution": "A sandbox that imitates remittance rails faithfully — transfers, agent holds, payouts, failures, reversals, webhooks, and reconciliation — with fake money and realistic messiness. Builders integrate against it exactly as they would a real rail; agents can trial tools with zero risk; and every project that survives it produces evidence: audit trails, reconciliation reports, and a completed security review. We are building the practice field the whole ecosystem shares, shaped directly by people who run counters, ports, and security reviews.",
    "traction": "v0.1 of the test API is live with signed webhooks and a full audit trail. The Lab has an active core team spanning engineering, security, design, and logistics, contributing from four countries. Sixty realistic test fixtures based on real port-reconciliation patterns run against the API, community members outside the Lab are already requesting test keys, and a xawilaad agent with thirty years behind the counter has offered his shop for field observation — the kind of domain access remittance startups usually cannot buy.",
    "team": "Led by Ayaan Xuseen Warsame (Hargeisa) — ex-remittance operations, self-taught engineer, Lab lead. Core team: Nasra Ibraahim Cali (Stockholm), application-security engineer who owns the release review gate; Sagal Maxamuud Diiriye (Toronto), product designer focused on first-time and low-bandwidth users; Cawo Daahir Samatar (Mogadishu), freight-reconciliation specialist translating port-operations discipline into payments; and Hamdi Yuusuf Cige (Jigjiga), keeping the build offline-first and honest for rural users.",
